/**
  @page examples-pdes-periodic Poisson equation with periodic boundary conditions
  @brief Identify opposite boundaries and impose a zero-mean normalization.

  Periodic boundaries identify opposite edges of a computational cell. Unlike
  the @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Dirichlet Poisson problem", this problem has
  no fixed boundary value: the solution is only determined up to a constant.
  This example handles both the periodic identification and that remaining
  freedom explicitly.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-periodic-intro The mathematical problem

  On @f$\Omega=(0,2\pi)^2@f$, solve
  @f[
    -\Delta u=2\sin x\cos y,
    \qquad u\text{ and }\nabla u\text{ are periodic across opposite edges},
    \qquad \int_\Omega u\,dx=0.
  @f]
  The source has zero integral, as required by compatibility of a periodic
  Poisson problem. The zero-mean normalization selects the unique solution
  @f$u_{\mathrm{ex}}(x,y)=\sin x\cos y@f$.

  In the periodic finite element space, a global multiplier @f$\lambda\in\mathbb R@f$
  enforces the mean condition. The weak problem is
  @f[
    \int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dx+\lambda\int_\Omega v\,dx
      =\int_\Omega f v\,dx,
    \qquad \eta\int_\Omega u\,dx=0
  @f]
  for every periodic test function @f$v@f$ and scalar test value @f$\eta@f$.
  This removes the constant nullspace without changing the PDE on compatible
  data. The normalization removes the constant kernel before factorization.

  @section examples-pdes-periodic-mesh 1. Construct the periodic cell

  The square is discretized with 33 vertices per direction, giving 1089 vertices
  and 2048 triangles before periodic identification.

  @snippet PDEs/Periodic.cpp periodic-mesh

  @section examples-pdes-periodic-dofmap 2. Pair boundary degrees of freedom

  For scalar `P1`, each vertex has one degree of freedom. The map below obtains
  its index from the space, then uses the vertex coordinates to find its periodic
  representative. The indices are obtained from the finite element space.

  @snippet PDEs/Periodic.cpp periodic-map

  Vertices on the right edge map to the left edge, and those on the top map to
  the bottom. The top-right corner maps directly to the bottom-left corner, so
  no cyclic chain is introduced. Each entry means “this slave DOF equals this
  master DOF.” This pairing is specific to this matching scalar P1 grid; higher
  order spaces also require edge and possibly face DOFs to be paired.

  @section examples-pdes-periodic-problem 3. Impose periodicity and the mean

  `P0g` provides one constant over the entire mesh. Its trial
  function is the multiplier, while its test function imposes the mean equation.

  @snippet PDEs/Periodic.cpp periodic-form

  The periodic constraints identify both trial and test variations during
  assembly. Flux periodicity is the natural boundary condition of this weak
  formulation; no independent Dirichlet data are prescribed.

  @section examples-pdes-periodic-output 4. Solve and inspect the result

  The multiplier gives a nonsingular saddle-point system, solved here with
  sparse LU. The result is exported on the original square mesh, with matching
  values on the paired edges.

  @snippet PDEs/Periodic.cpp periodic-solve

  @image html PDEs/Periodic.png "Computed zero-mean solution. Opposite edges carry matching values." width=480px

  Open `Periodic.xdmf` and color by **u**. Periodic constraints are checked by comparing paired boundary values. The discrete
  integral verifies the zero-mean normalization, and comparison with
  @f$\sin x\cos y@f$ measures the field error.

  @section examples-pdes-periodic-full Complete program

  @snippet PDEs/Periodic.cpp periodic-source

  See @ref PDEs/Periodic.cpp "Periodic.cpp" and the
  @ref guides-boundary-conditions "boundary conditions guide".

  @example PDEs/Periodic.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-periodic,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
